{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "order_of_operations", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Widen first, filter second** - this is the correct order given the quadratic FP cost structure.", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "WIDEN FIRST, FILTER SECOND. A precision filter on today's forks caps at +0.0027 perfect / +0.0007 honest. Widening is nearly free at this operating point: FPs going 613 -> 1200 costs only -0.0003, because the quadratic FP cost only bites once precision is already high."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "postproc_ceiling", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**[MEASURED] 84 of 151 divisions (55.6 %) are recoverable by post-processing alone** ... the post-processing ceiling is 89/151 = 0.589 divJ.", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "84 of 151 divisions (55.6%) are recoverable by POST-PROCESSING ALONE - mother and both daughters are already detected and matched, only the second mother-daughter edge is missing. With the 5 existing TPs the post-processing ceiling is 89/151 = 0.589 divJ."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "public_field_inherited_the_bug", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Note this also explains why the public 0.918 stack's identical constants leave the whole public field at divJ~0: the entire lineage inherited the same mis-set gate.", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "The public 0.918 stack carries IDENTICAL constants, which is why the entire public field sits at divJ ~ 0. The whole lineage inherited the same mis-set gate."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "ranking_is_backwards", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "it ranks the tightest pairs first, i.e. exactly backwards with respect to the measured GT geometry", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "The proposer's ranking score = parent_dist + 0.15*sister_dist (wrapper.py:831) is monotonically INCREASING in both distances, so it ranks tightest pairs first - exactly backwards. True divisions are the wide, angularly-opposed, persistently-diverging ones."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "root_cause_gates", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The deployed division proposer's sister-distance gate (7.2 um) sits below the GT median inter-daughter distance in both embryos (8.98 / 11.47 um). We are not ranking true divisions badly - we are refusing to propose them.", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "THE BUG: the fork proposer's gates are mis-set against measured GT geometry. SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM=4.7 vs GT median parent-daughter 5.7-6.4um (p90 8.4-9.9); SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM=7.2 vs GT median inter-daughter 8.98um (44b6) / 11.47um (6bba). The gate excludes the MEDIAN true division. Located at src/biotrack/wrapper.py:123-127."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "separating_features", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "| `cos_angle` | [-0.99, -0.51] | <= -0.51 | **63.7 %** |", "review_after": null, "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "value": "cos_angle is the only feature with real one-sided power: it kills 63.7% of FP forks while retaining all 5 TPs. True divisions sit at cos -0.71..-0.76 (~135-140 deg); FP forks at -0.33. True sisters keep diverging (9.5um by t+2); FP forks stay at 5.3um. No temporal or depth gating opportunity exists - both are uniform in t and z."}
