{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "key": "colab_budget", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "T4: approximately 1.76 compute units per hour; A100: approximately 15 compute units per hour", "review_after": "2026-12-11", "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "hardware", "value": "~1000 Colab compute units. Burn rates approx: A100 ~12-15 units/hr, L4 ~4.8/hr, T4 ~1.76/hr. So the budget is roughly 65-85 h of A100, ~200 h of L4, or ~500 h of T4. A100 is ~10x the M5 Pro's estimated FP16 and ~5x its memory bandwidth (1555 vs 307 GB/s)."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "colab_cli_supersedes_relay", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "colab run [--gpu GPU] ... Run a local script on a fresh VM, forwarding arguments, then release it", "review_after": "2026-12-11", "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "hardware", "value": "googlecolab/google-colab-cli (Apache-2.0, official Google org, v0.6.0) replaces the hand-rolled git-relay entirely: colab new --gpu A100 / colab run script.py / colab download / colab stop, plus a keep-alive daemon. Installed to ~/.local/bin/colab via a venv (Arch blocks pip per PEP 668). It does NOT expose compute-unit accounting - `colab pay` only opens the billing page - so the relay's per-job unit tracking has no CLI equivalent."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "key": "colab_measured_burn_rate", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "session: 8.2 units, 6.0 h, ['smoke', 'notebook']", "review_after": "2026-12-11", "status": "active", "tbl": "fact", "topic": "hardware", "value": "MEASURED on this account 2026-08-26, better than any published rate: four Colab sessions each ran 6.0 h and consumed 8.2 / 8.4 / 9.85 / 9.9 units - about 1.37-1.65 units/hour. That is T4-class consumption. ~1000 units is therefore roughly 600-700 h of T4, NOT the A100 figure; A100 burn must be measured separately before planning around it."}
