{"choice": "Hybrid: build an honest detector+linker, then TUNE the detection threshold on the node-count ratio as a legitimate calibrated parameter. Do not ship a pure hack.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Do we ride the node-count 'metric hack' to the top of the public LB?", "rationale": "The adjustment is part of the published metric and calibrating N_pred is legitimate tuning. But a submission whose ONLY source of score is metric gaming is exposed by the winner obligations (training+inference code, reproducible methodology, recorded call) and by rules 3.148 (unfair playing practices). The host has already patched one exploit mid-competition and rescored. Pure-hack notebooks are also 7-minute runs with no model to defend.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Upload weights as a Kaggle Dataset/Model; notebook runs with internet off and loads them.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Train on Mac, infer on Kaggle - how do weights cross?", "rationale": "onlyAllowKernelSubmissions=True and internet is disabled in the notebook, so the Mac can never touch the submission path directly.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Candidates: the community 18.5GB synthetic set with 165k labelled divisions, Zebrahub, Cell Tracking Challenge.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which external data do we add?", "rationale": "Divisions are ~1 in 853 links and carry the 0.1 weight; they are the scarcest supervision. External division-rich data is the cleanest legitimate edge. Must confirm each source is free + public.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Decide before 2026-09-22 (team merger deadline).", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Team size - do we recruit?", "rationale": "maxTeamSize=5. Top-10 teams are mostly 2 members but two 6-member teams sit at rank 10-11 with 300+ submissions. Submissions are per-team at 5/day, so a bigger team does NOT buy more submissions.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Select one high-public-LB and one robust/low-variance run.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which 2 final submissions do we select?", "rationale": "numScoredSubmissions=2 and public LB is only 29% of test; the 0.946-0.947 pile means ties break on earliest submission and a shakeup is near-certain.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Division detection. Drive division_jaccard from ~0 toward 0.25+ on top of a solid edge baseline.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the primary honest lever to rank 1?", "rationale": "Gap/weight arithmetic puts the implied rank-1 division_jaccard at ~0.235, matching a reported 0.22. Divisions are ~1 link in 853 so this is a rare-event detection problem, which is exactly what the community 18.5GB synthetic set with 165k labelled divisions is for. Prior campaigns never closed this.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Probably yes for data prep, evaluation, packaging and the 98GB download - but measure a SUSTAINED multi-hour load before trusting it for training.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Is the HP laptop usable as a second machine during the sprint?", "rationale": "The handoff's 'fans are dead / 1.4 GHz' premise is contradicted by later measurement. If true, Biohub-X's bounded streaming cache and resume machinery were solving a problem that did not exist, and we gain a second machine for the sprint.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Kaggle T4 x2 for long training; the Mac for data prep, ablations, evaluation and packaging - run both at once.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Where do the long training runs actually go?", "rationale": "M5 Pro is ~33-35 TFLOPS dense FP16 (est.) vs a single T4's 65, so Kaggle's T4x2 is ~4x the Mac for raw throughput, and bandwidth is at parity (307 vs 300 GB/s). The Mac's real edge is 48GB unified with no copies and no 12h wall. Plan patch size for the 16GB T4, not the 48GB Mac, or inference will not fit.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Upsample + Conv3d. Never ConvTranspose3d.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Detector decoder architecture?", "rationale": "ConvTranspose3d on MPS rejects fp16/bf16 and crashes autocast; fix PR still open. Costs nothing in model quality and removes the only hard MPS blocker.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "NO. One framework: PyTorch, device selected at runtime (mps / cuda / cpu).", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Do we adopt MLX for the Mac GPU, alongside CUDA for Kaggle?", "rationale": "Apple states PyTorch already reaches the M5 Neural Accelerators under the hood, so MLX is NOT required to use the 20-core GPU. MLX cannot produce a submission (Kaggle has no Metal); torch 2.14 erased its one real advantage by taking conv3d to 97% of matmul peak; an open MLX issue has it 10-20% SLOWER than PyTorch on M5 bf16 GEMM; and a dual stack costs two implementations plus numerical-agreement checks in an 18-day runway. The sprint handoff had already decided this independently.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Colab A100 for heavy training; Kaggle T4x2 for free parallel runs and submission-identical numerics; Mac for everything with no marginal cost.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which machine does the heavy training?", "rationale": "The Mac is the slowest of the three for raw training. Colab A100 is ~10x it. The Mac's value is zero-cost unlimited iteration, 48GB, and no disconnects - so it owns data prep, the classical baseline, metric work, ablations, evaluation and packaging.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "No. Only the detector is GPU-bound. Linking is combinatorial (bipartite matching / ILP) and CPU-bound, and the podium gap is the division term, which is a rare-event modelling problem.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Is GPU compute actually our binding constraint?", "rationale": "Adding A100 hours cannot fix a model that does not detect divisions. Budget compute to the detector and spend thinking time on divisions.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Re-gate the division fork proposer: SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM 4.7->10, SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM 7.2->15, and replace the ranking score with one DECREASING in divisionness (-cos_angle + lambda*divergence persistence).", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the single highest-value action in the sprint?", "rationale": "Measured: current gates admit only 10 of 89 metric-reachable divisions; widened they admit 81. fork_at_matched_mother is literally 0 today. CPU-only, hours, no retrain, no GPU, no new data. The entire public field carries the same constants, which is why the 0.9465 plateau has divJ~0 - so this is both the gap AND a differentiator nobody else has fixed.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Yes - archived (read-only), not deleted. Its measured burn rates and parity-failure lessons are extracted into the base.", "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Retire biohub-colab-relay now that the official Colab CLI works?", "rationale": "The CLI supersedes every function the relay performed and is maintained by Google. Deleting would destroy the 5 parity-failure notebooks and the only measured unit data on this account, for zero benefit - archiving already stops it being used. The one capability lost is per-job unit accounting, which the CLI does not provide.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
